// A promise settled from OUTSIDE gametick dispatch must be handed to the
// event loop, not to the tick queue.
//
// The microtask drain is armed by the settle. Inside gametick dispatch a
// delay-0 gametick event is the right vehicle: call_tick_events() re-checks
// its queue after every callback, so the drain runs in that same pass. But
// call_tick_events() is only ever entered from backend_run_one_gametick(),
// so the same event armed from anywhere ELSE -- a walltime callback, a
// socket read -- is not looked at again until the next gametick.
//
// That is the path every package/async I/O completion takes: async_read,
// async_write, async_getdir and async_db_exec all settle their promise from
// a walltime callback. Each of them used to wait up to a full gametick (a
// second at the default 1000ms) before its handler ran, on work that had
// already finished. Measured in isolation on the unfixed driver: 101ms
// against a 100ms gametick, i.e. exactly one tick; sub-millisecond fixed.
//
// KNOWN LIMIT: drain_arms_loop is driver-global, so in a full suite run
// another file's I/O completion landing inside this file's window could
// satisfy the check on its own. That makes this an exact measurement only in
// single-file mode and a lower bound in a full run -- which is still enough
// for the regression it guards, since arming every external settle through
// the tick queue would leave the counter at zero in every run. Asserting a
// companion "did not arm through the tick queue" counter instead would be
// flaky for the mirror-image reason: another file settling inside gametick
// dispatch would move it.
//
// The check is the "drain_arms_loop" counter rather than elapsed time.
// Wall-clock latency looks like the obvious test and is not usable here: it
// measures the QUEUE, not the arming decision. A correctly armed delivery
// still waits behind whatever else is pending, so in a full suite run --
// where other files leave chains and thousands of abandoned promises in
// flight -- this same test measured 330ms on one build and 83ms on another
// while the scheduler was behaving perfectly. The counter is exact,
// load-independent, and works on a host-driven virtual clock where elapsed
// time means nothing at all.

#ifdef __PACKAGE_ASYNC__
nosave int arms_before;
nosave int delivered;

private void check_delivery() {
  mapping info = async_info(1);

  ASSERT2(delivered, "async_read promise never delivered");
  ASSERT2(info["drain_arms_loop"] > arms_before,
    "an async_read completion armed the drain through the gametick queue: its handler "
    "waits for a tick boundary (up to a full gametick) instead of the event loop");
  RELEASE_LATCH("async delivery latency");
}

private void start_read() {
  arms_before = async_info(1)["drain_arms_loop"];
  promise_then(async_read("/single/tests/efuns/async_delivery_latency.lpc"),
    function(mixed data) {
      delivered = 1;
      // Checked from the delivery itself. A second call_out would add a
      // deadline, and a deadline measures the delivery QUEUE rather than the
      // arming decision -- under a small `async drain batch`, or a gametick
      // long enough to collapse two call_outs into one tick, a perfectly
      // armed delivery arrives after it.
      check_delivery();
      return data;
    });
}
#endif

void do_tests() {
#ifdef __PACKAGE_ASYNC__
  EXPECT_LATCH("async delivery latency");

  // Started from a call_out so the ordering is call_out-to-call_out, which is
  // defined; the suite holds the stack until it finishes either way.
  call_out((: start_read :), 1);
#endif
}
